=begin pod :kind("Type") :subkind("class") :category("basic")

=TITLE package RakuAST::Name

=SUBTITLE Represent an identifier name

    class RakuAST::Name { }

The C<RakuAST::Name> class represents a name (usually an identifier,
but not necessarily so), which consists of zero or more parts, and may
have additional colonpair (adverb) specification(s).

=head2 Object introspection

C<RakuAST::Name> objects are typically created when parsing Raku
Programming Language code.  So most developers will only need to know
how to introspect the objects created.

These introspection methods are available (in alphabetical order):

=head3 base-name

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
dd $name.base-name;
=end code

The C<base-name> method returns a clone of the given C<RakuAST::Name>
object, but with the last part removed B<if> the last part is empty.

=head3 canonicalize

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.canonicalize;
=end code

The C<canonicalize> returns a string representing the given
C<RakuAST::Name object>, or dies when it is impossible to create a
string because there are still unresolved parts.

=head3 colonpairs

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
.say for $name.colonpairs;
=end code

The C<colonpairs> method returns the "colonpairs" (aka "adverbs") of
the C<RakuAST::Name> object as C<RakuAST::ColonPair> objects.  For an
identifier such as "JSON::Fast:ver<0.21>:auth<zef:timo>", it would return
L<C<RakuAST::ColonPair>|/type/RakuAST/ColonPair> objects for 
C<:ver<0.21>> and C<:auth<zef:timo>>.

=head3 first-colonpair

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
dd $name.first-colonpair("ver");
=end code

The C<first-colonpair> method returns the first C<RakuAST::ColonPair::xxx>
object that matches the given name.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 first-part

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.first-part;
=end code

The C<first-part> method returns the first "name" part of the
C<RakuAST::Name> object as a C<RakuAST::Name::Part> object.  For an
identifier such as "IO::Path", it would return a
L<C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple>|/type/RakuAST/Name/Part/Simple> object
representing "IO".

=head3 has-colonpair

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.has-colonpair("ver");  # True or False
=end code

The C<has-colonpair> method returns C<True> if there is at least one
colonpair that has the given name.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 has-colonpairs

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.has-colonpairs;  # True or False
=end code

The C<has-colonpairs> method returns C<True> if there either are any
colonpairs in the C<RakuAST::Name> object.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-anonymous

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-anonymous;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-anonymous> method returns C<True> if there either are two parts
and both are empty.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-empty

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-empty;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-empty> method returns C<True> if there either are no parts,
or the only part is empty.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-global-lookup

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-global-lookup;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-global-lookup> method returns C<True> if the first part is
a C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple> object, and its name is "GLOBAL".
Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-identifier

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-identifier;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-identifier> method returns C<True> if the name can be considered
a valid identifier.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-indirect-lookup

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-indirect-lookup;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-indirect-lookup> method returns C<True> if B<any> part of the
C<RakuAST::Name> object is a C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Expression> object.
Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-installable

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-installable;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-installable> method returns C<True> if the name can be considered
a valid, installable identifier.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-multi-part

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-multi-part;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-multi-part> method returns C<True> if there is more than one
B<non-empty> part in the C<RakuAST::Name> object.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-package-lookup

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-package-lookup;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-package-lookup> method returns C<True> if the B<last> part is a
C<RakuAST::Name::Part::EmptyEdge> part.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-package-search

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-package-search;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-package-search> method returns C<True> if the B<first> part is a
C<RakuAST::Name::Part::EmptyEdge> part.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-pseudo-package

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-pseudo-package;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-pseudo-package> method returns C<True> if the C<RakuAST::Name>
object represents a pseudo-package.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-simple

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-simple;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-simple> method returns C<False> if there is any
non-C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple> part.  Else it returns C<True>.

=head3 last-part

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.last-part;
=end code

The C<last-part> method returns the last "name" part of the
C<RakuAST::Name> object as a C<RakuAST::Name::Part> object.  For an
identifier such as "IO::Path", it would return a
L<C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple>|/type/RakuAST/Name/Part/Simple> object
representing "Path".

=head3 parts

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
.say for $name.parts;
=end code

The C<parts> method returns the "name" parts of the C<RakuAST::Name>
object as C<RakuAST::Name::Part> objects.  For an identifier such as
"IO::Path", it would return
L<C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple>|/type/RakuAST/Name/Part/Simple> objects
for "IO" and "Path".

=head3 root-part

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.root-part;
=end code

The C<root-part> method returns the root "name" part of the
C<RakuAST::Name> object as a C<RakuAST::Name::Part> object. It only
differs from C<first-part> if the first part of the name is empty.
For an identifier such as "::Foo", it would return a
L<C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple>|/type/RakuAST/Name/Part/Simple> object
representing "Foo".

=head3 simple-identifier

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
if $name.simple-identifier -> $identifier {
    say $identifier;
}
else {
    say "not a simple identifier";
}
=end code

The C<simple-identifier> method returns the string of a simple
one-part identifier, or the empty string.

=head3 without-colonpair

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
my $without = $name.without-colonpair("ver");
=end code

The C<without-colonpair> method returns a clone of the given
C<RakuAST::Name> object B<without> any C<RakuAST::ColonPair::xxx>
object that matches the given name.

=head3 without-first-part

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
my $without = $name.without-first-part;
=end code

The C<without-colonpair> method returns a clone of the given
C<RakuAST::Name> object B<without> the first part.

=head3 first-part

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.first-part;
=end code

The C<first-part> method returns the first "name" part of the
C<RakuAST::Name> object as a C<RakuAST::Name::Part> object.  For an
identifier such as "IO::Path", it would return a
L<C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple>|/type/RakuAST/Name/Part/Simple> object
representing "IO".

=head3 has-colonpair

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.has-colonpair("ver");  # True or False
=end code

The C<has-colonpair> method returns C<True> if there is at least one
colonpair that has the given name.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 has-colonpairs

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.has-colonpairs;  # True or False
=end code

The C<has-colonpairs> method returns C<True> if there either are any
colonpairs in the C<RakuAST::Name> object.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-anonymous

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-anonymous;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-anonymous> method returns C<True> if there either are two parts
and both are empty.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-empty

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-empty;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-empty> method returns C<True> if there either are no parts,
or the only part is empty.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-identifier

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-identifier;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-identifier> method returns C<True> if the name can be considered
a valid identifier.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-indirect-lookup

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-indirect-lookup;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-indirect-lookup> method returns C<True> if B<any> part of the
C<RakuAST::Name> object is a C<RakuAST::Name::Part::Expression> object.
Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-installable

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-installable;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-installable> method returns C<True> if the name can be considered
a valid, installable identifier.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-multi-part

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-multi-part;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-multi-part> method returns C<True> if there is more than one
B<non-empty> part in the C<RakuAST::Name> object.  Else it returns C<False>.

=head3 is-package-lookup

=begin code :preamble<my $name>
say $name.is-package-lookup;  # True or False

=head2 Object creation

There are several ways to create a C<RakuAST::Name> object.  For simple
identifiers (without adverbs, aka "colonpairs") it is recommended to use
the alternate C<.from-identifier> and C<.from-identifier-parts> methods
to create a C<RakuAST::Name> object.  Otherwise one can use the standard
C<.new> method.

=head3 new

=begin code
my $name = RakuAST::Name.new(
  RakuAST::Name::Part::Simple.new("Foo"),
  colonpairs => (
    RakuAST::ColonPair::Value.new(
      key   => "ver",
      value => RakuAST::StrLiteral.new("1.1")
    ),
  )
);
dd $name;  # Foo:ver("1.1")
=end code

Creates a C<RakuAST::Name> object from the given positional arguments
(the name parts, which should be C<RakuAST::Name::Part::xxx> obbjects)
and an optional list of C<RakuAST::ColonPair::xxx> objects (which
represent the "adverbs" on the name).

=head3 from-identifier

=begin code
my $name = RakuAST::Name.from-identifier('$foo');  # $foo
dd $name;  # $foo
=end code

Creates a C<RakuAST::Name> object from the given simple identifier
string (which may B<not> contain any C<::> separators).

=head3 from-identifier-parts

=begin code
my $name = RakuAST::Name.from-identifier-parts('IO','Path');  # IO::Path
dd $name;  # IO::Path
=end code

Creates a C<RakuAST::Name> object from the given simple identifier
string parts (which may B<not> contain any C<::> separators).

=head2 Object modifiers

These methods can be used to change an existing C<RakuAST::Name> object.

=head3 add-colonpair

=begin code
my $name = RakuAST::Name.from-identifier("Foo");
$name.add-colonpair(
  RakuAST::ColonPair::Value.new(
    key   => "ver",
    value => RakuAST::StrLiteral.new("1.1")
  )
);
dd $name;  # Foo:ver("1.1")
=end code

Adds a C<RakuAST::ColonPair::xxx> object to the list of colonpairs.

=end pod
